{
  "type": "doc",
  "content": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "Time II: Months",
          "type": "text",
          "marks": [
            {
              "type": "bold"
            },
            {
              "type": "underline"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "hardBreak"
        },
        {
          "type": "hardBreak"
        },
        {
          "text": "Honestly, it is surprising that the names of months aren't written anywhere on the curriculum of the exams, but it will be explained here none the less.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "type": "hardBreak"
        },
        {
          "type": "hardBreak"
        },
        {
          "text": "A month is called a 月（read as ぐゎち）. It is the same kanji for moon 月（read as ちち）.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "type": "hardBreak"
        },
        {
          "text": "A month and date together is called a 月日（がっぴ） . This word is the same in Okinawan and Japanese.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "type": "hardBreak"
        },
        {
          "type": "hardBreak"
        },
        {
          "text": "As a friendly reminder, ぐゎ is read as \"gwa\".",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "type": "hardBreak"
        },
        {
          "type": "hardBreak"
        },
        {
          "text": "Count along:",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "type": "hardBreak"
        },
        {
          "text": "正月（そーぐゎち）= January",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "type": "hardBreak"
        },
        {
          "text": "二月（にんぐゎち）= February",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "type": "hardBreak"
        },
        {
          "text": "三月（さんぐゎち）= March",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "type": "hardBreak"
        },
        {
          "text": "四月（しんぐゎち）= April",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "type": "hardBreak"
        },
        {
          "text": "五月（ぐんぐゎち）= May",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "type": "hardBreak"
        },
        {
          "text": "六月（るくぐゎち）= June",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "type": "hardBreak"
        },
        {
          "text": "七月（しちぐゎち）= July",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "type": "hardBreak"
        },
        {
          "text": "八月（はちぐゎち）= August",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "type": "hardBreak"
        },
        {
          "text": "九月（くんぐゎち）= September",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "type": "hardBreak"
        },
        {
          "text": "十月（じゅーぐゎっち）= October",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "type": "hardBreak"
        },
        {
          "text": "霜月（しむちち）= November",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "type": "hardBreak"
        },
        {
          "text": "師走（しわーし）= December",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "type": "hardBreak"
        },
        {
          "type": "hardBreak"
        },
        {
          "text": "Do note however, dates and months in Okinawan without explanation usually indicate the old Japonic calendar system called 旧暦 in Japanese and doesn't have a name in Okinawan because that is the normal calendar system. The modern calendar system that the world uses is called either",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "type": "hardBreak"
        },
        {
          "type": "hardBreak"
        },
        {
          "text": "うらんだぐゆみ / うらんらぐゆみ or やまとぅぐゆみ. くゆみ means a calendar system (it becomes ぐゆみ because there is a word in front of it). うらんだ / うらんら means Holland / the Netherlands / the West in general. やまとぅ (written as 大和 in kanji) means (mainland) Japan. So, if you want to say that it's in the global calendar, say one of those words + ぬ which is the possessive / genitive (approx. \" 's \" in English). This will be elaborated on later.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "type": "hardBreak"
        },
        {
          "type": "hardBreak"
        },
        {
          "text": "Anyways, the normal calendar system has 閏月（ゆんぢち）which translate to leap month. So you might have a 閏６月（ゆんるくぐゎち）like there was in 2025. The month comes first, then the date. Like Japanese, the system is year, then month, then date.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "type": "hardBreak"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph"
    }
  ]
}
